<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jayavarman I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died c. 681</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jayavarman I was a king of Chenla in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> in the second half of the 7th century, the last ruler to hold the kingdom together. His inscriptions show his authority reaching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> delta into southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>. After his death around 681, leaving a daughter, Jayadevi, as his successor, Chenla broke up into rival principalities that Chinese sources called Land Chenla and Water Chenla.</p></div>
